WEBVTT

00:00:00.556 --> 00:00:24.460
<v Casey>Hey there, good to have you. It's Sunday, so we're looking up with Sky and Cosmos today. On today's episode we've got Webb pulling a known planet out of old data, a 115-million-year-old dinosaur bone that may hold traces of blood chemistry, and octopuses squeezing through gaps narrower than their eyes. Plus the Deep Dive: when a team recovers a planet from archive data, have they made a discovery, or found a lead? Stick around.

00:00:37.935 --> 00:00:56.175
<v Casey>Welcome to Cariboo Signals, an AI-generated review of the latest tech news and ideas that impact our rural communities. I'm Casey. It's Sunday, November 8th, 2026. Today's theme is Science, Wonder &amp; the Natural World. Reaching you from the heart of the Cariboo, the homeland of the Secwépemc, Tŝilhqot'in, and Dakelh nations.

00:00:57.175 --> 00:01:29.605
<v Riley>And I'm Riley. Today we're looking up, at what counts as a discovery when the data was collected years ago. Out at Horsefly Lake this morning, it's mainly clear and around 5 degrees, with a high of 11 and a low of 2. Moderate drizzle moves in through the day, with a 92 percent chance of precipitation, about 2 millimetres. Across the Cariboo, 100 Mile House is around 3, Williams Lake and Quesnel are around 6, and Riske Creek is around 2. Everywhere is partly cloudy except Riske Creek, which is mainly clear.

00:01:29.805 --> 00:01:30.861
<v Casey>Starting the news now.

00:01:32.261 --> 00:01:56.117
<v Casey>It's a mixed news day. The local stories sit close to home, and the science reaches a long way out. Williams Lake's 2026 to 2030 mayor and council were sworn in on Tuesday, November 3rd, at a packed ceremony. Williams Lake First Nation Kukpi7 Willie Sellars thanked them for holding up their end of the relationship. He said the progress reaches past city limits into the Cariboo Regional District.

00:01:56.567 --> 00:02:01.871
<v Riley>A dhol drummer and a highland piper led the procession. Most council swearings-in start more quietly.

00:02:03.501 --> 00:02:28.685
<v Riley>B.C.'s forestry leaders are welcoming Ottawa's $6.5 billion in federal funding, but they're not calling it a fix. The BC Council of Forest Industries says the money runs over six years, beginning in 2027/2028, for operations, facility upgrades and new forest products. Kim Haakstad, the council's president, says financial support alone won't solve the sector's competitiveness problem.

00:02:29.135 --> 00:02:32.511
<v Casey>Six years of money, and the program details still aren't settled.

00:02:34.180 --> 00:03:14.738
<v Casey>Going back a bit further. A team led by Curtin University reports heme, the iron-bearing molecule that lets hemoglobin carry oxygen, in a 115-million-year-old dinosaur leg bone from northeastern Brazil. The fossil is an Aratasaurus museunacionali. New Atlas and ScienceDaily both carried the study, which appeared in the Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences. It describes cell-like structures inside the bone's vascular canals, and the surrounding sediment lacked the same heme signature. Science News quotes paleontologist Mary Schweitzer of North Carolina State University, who was not part of the study, saying she is not convinced. Her objection is that the chemical analysis covered the whole bone.

00:03:15.187 --> 00:03:18.979
<v Riley>A signal from the whole bone can't yet be pinned on the cells themselves.

00:03:20.642 --> 00:03:32.602
<v Riley>Canada is adding more than a dozen aquatic species to the Species at Risk Act, including small B.C. sharks. The Tyee calls the move pretty rare. The listing puts those creatures under federal protection.

00:03:34.020 --> 00:03:55.764
<v Casey>Also in the water, a fish that's become a warning sign. Scientist Adalberto Luis Val has spent decades on the tambaqui, a fish in Brazil's Amazon. He studies how fish pull oxygen from water and how climate disruption cuts into that. The Guardian frames the tambaqui as a canary for climate upheaval, with implications for biodiversity and food security. Val, now 70, works out of Manaus.

00:03:57.278 --> 00:04:22.398
<v Riley>An Aarhus University-led study finds Europe was dominated for more than 20 million years by open woodland, grassy clearings and wildflower meadows. Large grazing animals kept trees and shrubs from taking over. The paper, in Biological Conservation, argues the dense dark forests common today are a relatively recent development. The authors say that raises important questions for modern reforestation programs.

00:04:23.866 --> 00:04:41.266
<v Casey>Field scientists in the Ethiopian highlands documented a bull and a male donkey bonding over several weeks near Debre Libanos. They mated repeatedly, nipped, licked and grazed side by side under sycamore trees. When the team took the story back to Italy, their students named the pair after characters from Homer.

00:04:41.716 --> 00:04:46.500
<v Riley>Achilles and Patroclo, from the Iliad. Somebody in that lab has read their Homer.

00:04:48.300 --> 00:05:09.260
<v Riley>A study in the journal Genes found evidence of natural selection in the dogs living around the Chernobyl plant. Dogs in the town of Chernobyl, about 16 kilometres away, are more genetically similar to dogs in the Galápagos than to the dogs at the plant. What drove the differences, and what advantage they give, is still an open question.

00:05:09.709 --> 00:05:13.781
<v Casey>Radiation is the tempting explanation, and the study stops short of naming it.

00:05:15.475 --> 00:05:40.755
<v Casey>Research in Astronomy &amp; Astrophysics suggests Neptune may have formed from material that started closer to the young Sun before moving outward. SciTechDaily reports the team found Neptune's deep atmosphere is considerably richer in oxygen than Uranus's, despite the two planets' similar size and mass. They inferred that from carbon monoxide measurements, including recent ALMA data, and from atmospheric models, because the deep atmospheres can't be sampled directly.

00:05:42.338 --> 00:06:13.728
<v Riley>Researchers writing in the Journal of Experimental Biology filmed common octopuses squeezing through small openings. When a gap is narrower than the distance between the animal's eyes, they add a step. They pass the remaining arms through first, then squeeze the mantle and head through separately. The study used three subadult common octopuses, and the researchers say the results can't be generalized. The work could inform soft robots for search and rescue, where a machine needs flexible arms that can pull its body through.

00:06:14.178 --> 00:06:18.586
<v Casey>Three animals, and the researchers flagged it themselves. That's the right amount of caution.

00:06:20.335 --> 00:06:44.999
<v Casey>Earth's rock record has a gap that geologists first noticed on several continents in the 1870s. The Great Unconformity can span hundreds of millions of years, and in places up to a billion. Research published in Nature says deep Earth processes lifted some continents above sea level, and then huge ice sheets scoured the rock away. Scientific American ran the explanation from researchers Santosh Dhungana and Nicholas Flament.

00:06:46.465 --> 00:07:14.001
<v Riley>Earlier this week, a University of Toronto-led study drew attention for a finding that cuts against decades of dairy advice. Adults who ate three servings of full-fat dairy a day for 12 weeks saw no harm to weight, cholesterol or insulin resistance, compared with a diet containing little dairy. The study, led by professor Harvey Anderson, appeared in The Journal of Nutrition. Participants who ate more dairy also showed improved blood pressure.

00:07:15.435 --> 00:07:34.387
<v Casey>At CERN, physicists working with the Large Hadron Collider found signals that a neutral B meson decayed into a muon and an antimuon. Physicists have hoped to see that rare decay for more than four decades. WIRED describes it as a first step toward exploring the zeptoverse, a scale about 100 billion times smaller than an atom.

00:07:35.842 --> 00:08:05.074
<v Riley>Astronomers led by a Northwestern team have developed a physics-based method called FLIP to strip residual starlight from James Webb Space Telescope images. It improves sensitivity to faint planets by two to 15 times, uses observations Webb already made, and needs no hardware change. It revealed the known giant planet 51 Eridani b at a wavelength where earlier processing failed. The team is now reprocessing hundreds of coronagraphic images to look for more planets.

00:08:06.676 --> 00:08:34.268
<v Casey>The World Meteorological Organization reports that carbon dioxide, methane and nitrous oxide all reached record atmospheric levels in 2025. Carbon dioxide averaged 426.8 parts per million, methane 1,948 parts per billion, and nitrous oxide 339.1 parts per billion. The bulletin measures what has built up in the air, which is different from what was emitted during the year. The WMO says the warming effect will last for decades or centuries.

00:08:34.718 --> 00:08:42.502
<v Riley>That CO2 figure works out to roughly one molecule in every 2,300 in the air. A small share with a very long memory.

00:08:44.277 --> 00:09:19.725
<v Riley>The 2026 Lancet Countdown report, from 146 researchers in 30 countries, estimates heat disruption cost the world $1.07 trillion in lost worker productivity in 2025. Extreme drought affected about 52 percent of global land for at least a month. The researchers urge governments to treat climate change as a global health emergency. Clean-energy investment reached $2.1 trillion in 2025, nearly twice fossil-fuel investment, yet fossil fuels still supplied more than 80 percent of global energy.

00:09:21.071 --> 00:09:33.647
<v Casey>Several of today's findings rest on something indirect: a model, a reprocessed image, a signal pulled out of noise. How far to trust a result nobody can check directly comes up again in a few minutes. First, a local organization.

00:09:35.047 --> 00:09:50.511
<v Riley>Quick meta moment before we move on. The roundup now groups stories by discipline inside each cluster, not just by the broad group. So a thing about grid batteries and a thing about pipeline permits no longer sit together just because they share a region.

00:09:51.036 --> 00:10:02.108
<v Casey>Good. I used to hear two unrelated stories back to back and wonder what the producers were smuggling in. The thing I'd want to know is whether it holds when a story genuinely straddles two disciplines.

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:09.320
<v Riley>It falls to whichever discipline dominates, so there's a judgment call in there. That's a caveat, not a flaw.

00:10:09.974 --> 00:10:15.646
<v Casey>Fair. Next one: the cold open now names the super-cycle focus, but only once the script actually gets to it.

00:10:16.258 --> 00:10:24.138
<v Riley>Which is the part I like. Before, the intro could promise a topic the episode never reached. Now the gate checks the script first.

00:10:24.650 --> 00:10:31.794
<v Casey>So if the script drifts, the intro quietly stays quiet. That's the right instinct, though I'll only believe it after a few episodes.

00:10:32.147 --> 00:10:36.419
<v Riley>Fine, a fair test. And the cuts are now named, so the reader can grade them.

00:10:36.959 --> 00:10:43.543
<v Casey>That one I'll give a standing ovation. Knowing what got dropped and why is the whole accountability question I keep asking.

00:10:44.126 --> 00:10:52.270
<v Riley>And the charter-fit change means a stray keyword can't override a story that clearly doesn't belong, in the roundup or the deep-dive pick.

00:10:52.603 --> 00:10:56.259
<v Casey>Less hype, more fit. Good. Right, let's get into the rest of the show.

00:10:57.659 --> 00:11:26.585
<v Casey>Central Chilcotin Rehabilitation Ltd. is an Indigenous joint venture doing forest rehabilitation and biomass work across the Tŝideldel, Tl'etinqox and Yunesit'in territories. The work has won awards, and it puts Indigenous leadership at the centre of what happens to the land after the cutting is done. Replanting, clearing leftover wood, turning slash into biomass instead of burn piles: those are the decisions that shape a landscape for decades. If you want a working example of local ownership, follow this one.

00:11:27.134 --> 00:11:33.286
<v Riley>Rehabilitation is unglamorous work, and it decides whether a landscape is still usable fifty years from now.

00:11:33.965 --> 00:11:38.381
<v Casey>Over to the sky, and to a results question the astronomers themselves haven't settled.

00:11:39.781 --> 00:11:45.677
<v Casey>When a team pulls a planet out of Webb data that was collected years ago, have they made a discovery, or found a lead?

00:11:46.077 --> 00:12:11.181
<v Riley>A discovery, if the method holds up. FLIP builds what the team calls a digital twin of Webb's NIRCam instrument, a software model of the optics. It corrects for the small ways a reference star's image fails to match the science image. And because it works from the archive, the gain is measured against the old processing on the same frames. Two to 15 times more sensitive is a number you can check today.

00:12:11.813 --> 00:12:23.109
<v Casey>The proof so far is a planet everyone already knew about. 51 Eridani b was found before FLIP touched it. Recovering it at a new wavelength is a good result. It tests recovery. It doesn't test finding.

00:12:23.678 --> 00:12:30.154
<v Riley>Recovery is how you calibrate a tool. A method has to see what's already there before anyone trusts it with what isn't.

00:12:30.154 --> 00:12:37.386
<v Casey>Sure. The sharper version of my objection: a pipeline tuned to show one planet can also be tuned to show what the team expects to see.

00:12:37.971 --> 00:12:51.435
<v Riley>Which is why astronomers usually test a pipeline by planting artificial planets in the data and checking whether it digs them back out. That's standard practice in direct imaging, and it's the test I'd want reported alongside FLIP.

00:12:51.945 --> 00:13:03.841
<v Casey>Planted planets only test what you thought to plant. A faint companion at an angle nobody modelled won't show up. The planting proves the method recovers what you put in. Finding what you didn't put in is a separate claim.

00:13:04.428 --> 00:13:11.516
<v Riley>It's a bounded test, and bounded still beats none. A method that fails the planting test should never reach the archive.

00:13:12.049 --> 00:13:18.785
<v Casey>On that we agree. The real test is the batch of hundreds of archive images now being rerun, and those results are still pending.

00:13:19.309 --> 00:13:27.845
<v Riley>And they'll arrive from data nobody has to fly again. A new mission takes years of engineering before it sees a photon. This takes computing.

00:13:28.380 --> 00:13:40.292
<v Casey>Computing somebody has to keep paying for. The team says further work is needed to cut computing costs and test the method on larger data sets. Who pays for hundreds of reruns, and who pays when the next batch lands?

00:13:40.554 --> 00:13:49.178
<v Riley>A server bill is much smaller than a launch. Northwestern, Caltech and MIT people built this on existing machines, without a single new mirror.

00:13:49.803 --> 00:13:55.619
<v Casey>A server bill that comes due every time the archive grows. Small numbers add up when nobody budgets them.

00:13:56.219 --> 00:14:16.507
<v Riley>The bigger gain may be what it frees up. Today, Webb spends time photographing other stars purely to build reference images for subtraction. If FLIP can eventually model the glare without those, that time goes to more stars and planets. Telescope time is the scarce resource on Webb. A saving there beats one sharper picture of one star.

00:14:17.100 --> 00:14:25.364
<v Casey>That saving is on paper. FLIP still calibrates against a reference Webb observation first, and skipping that stage is a goal with no date attached.

00:14:25.882 --> 00:14:35.098
<v Riley>The first stage is a known cost the team wants to remove. That's a design target, and targets get met or missed in public, where you can check which.

00:14:35.772 --> 00:14:53.924
<v Casey>Then there's the claim about future observatories. If software can correct for optical drift, the next telescope might not need to be built quite so rigidly stable. Might is the whole claim. A stability requirement relaxed on the strength of one method is a decision somebody defends ten years later, after the method has changed.

00:14:54.579 --> 00:15:06.995
<v Riley>Every method starts at might. What matters is whether the next observatory gets specified looser because someone showed this working on a real archive. That lever sits with the people writing the specifications.

00:15:07.285 --> 00:15:09.957
<v Casey>And those people get asked to fund the consequences later.

00:15:10.457 --> 00:15:19.881
<v Casey>Neptune is the harder case. That oxygen-rich origin story from up top rests on a model, and the outside check the researchers point to is NASA's Uranus Orbiter and Probe.

00:15:20.484 --> 00:15:37.956
<v Riley>Inference is how planetary science works when you can't reach the thing. The team measured a chemical difference between two planets of similar size and mass and built a formation story from it. The oxygen gap is the part a probe can test directly, which makes the claim testable at all.

00:15:38.305 --> 00:15:39.633
<v Casey>When does that probe launch?

00:15:39.901 --> 00:15:52.837
<v Riley>The earliest window is June 2031, with a backup in April 2032. NASA said in 2023 that the mid-to-late 2030s is more likely, because of plutonium shortfalls for its power source.

00:15:53.478 --> 00:16:02.569
<v Casey>So a launch, a cruise out past Saturn, the measurements, then the analysis. And Uranus only answers half the question. Waiting on it is no plan for being right any sooner.

00:16:02.569 --> 00:16:15.585
<v Riley>Then label it an inference, publish the model with the claim, and let the probe test it. The decadal survey ranked that mission the top large planetary priority for a reason. Holding the result for fifteen years helps no one.

00:16:16.228 --> 00:16:23.068
<v Casey>Labels are the first thing dropped. By the time a result is a headline, the model has fallen off and the claim sounds like a measurement.

00:16:23.594 --> 00:16:29.730
<v Riley>Then the label matters more when the wait is long. A label costs a sentence. A probe costs a launch vehicle.

00:16:30.059 --> 00:16:32.387
<v Casey>Who checks the label after the headline moves on?

00:16:32.702 --> 00:16:37.590
<v Riley>The probe, eventually. Until then, the label tells a reader which parts are models.

00:16:38.165 --> 00:16:45.493
<v Casey>And the Neptune inference goes years with no check from outside the team. I'd want that written into the press release in plain words.

00:16:45.992 --> 00:16:58.528
<v Riley>The CERN result is the same job at the opposite scale. Pulling a faint signal out of a noisy collision is pulling a planet out of a noisy telescope image. The decay is rare enough that seeing it at all counts as the result.

00:16:59.133 --> 00:17:08.901
<v Casey>The signal is the result. "First step" and "glimpse" are forecasts. WIRED uses them to open up a scale nobody has explored yet, and a reader can mistake a forecast for a finding.

00:17:09.536 --> 00:17:23.728
<v Riley>The physics is well defined, though. A muon is about 200 times heavier than an electron, and the neutral B meson lasts only a sliver of a second before it decays. After four decades of looking, a rate that low is hard to fake by accident.

00:17:24.305 --> 00:17:32.217
<v Casey>Rare enough that one signal in a huge dataset needs a strict statistical bar before anyone calls it real. That bar is what I'd want a reader told about.

00:17:32.795 --> 00:17:43.243
<v Riley>Particle physicists set that bar before they announce, and it's stricter than most fields use. A decay that rare, cleared against that bar, is what a first step looks like.

00:17:43.560 --> 00:17:47.104
<v Casey>Cut the adjectives, keep the signal. The zeptoverse is still a plan.

00:17:47.460 --> 00:17:51.748
<v Riley>A plan with a working detector behind it. Most plans don't get that footing.

00:17:52.091 --> 00:17:54.843
<v Casey>The detector I'll grant. The plan is still a plan.

00:17:55.343 --> 00:18:07.559
<v Casey>That leaves the sky you can look at yourself. The Cariboo has dark skies, and on a clear night out past Williams Lake, the light you see arrives unedited. Webb's view has been cleaned up by a model before it reaches a screen.

00:18:08.092 --> 00:18:31.252
<v Riley>Processing is what lets Webb show a faint planet beside a bright star. A backyard telescope can't do that on the darkest night of the year. But on a good night away from town lights, most people here can see the Milky Way with no instrument at all. That's how a lot of people come to care about any of this. A dark sky gets you into the subject. It doesn't compete with Webb as evidence.

00:18:31.875 --> 00:18:40.339
<v Casey>A clear night lets you recheck what you saw. You go out the next night and see whether it's still there. Nobody can recheck a reprocessed Webb image from a backyard.

00:18:40.639 --> 00:18:56.751
<v Riley>Then the check has to be a second team running the same method on the same archive. That fix is structural, and it needs no heroics. Many Webb programs release their data publicly after an exclusive period, with terms that vary by program, so the raw frames are already there.

00:18:57.365 --> 00:19:07.789
<v Casey>Sitting in an archive and ready to use are two different states. Someone needs the software, the calibration files, and time on a machine. Does a small team outside a big institution ever get that time?

00:19:08.054 --> 00:19:15.214
<v Riley>The team, the probe, or a second group. Each is a check, and none is free. Only a second group can tell the first one no.

00:19:15.804 --> 00:19:21.704
<v Casey>And if each assumes somebody else is checking, nobody is. I'd want that failure named in the next budget line.

00:19:21.704 --> 00:19:25.072
<v Riley>Name it, then fix it. The fix costs less than the failure.

00:19:25.411 --> 00:19:29.483
<v Casey>In my experience the archive gets the bill after the announcement, every time.

00:19:30.014 --> 00:19:38.558
<v Riley>So move the cost up front. Require the planted-planet test in the paper, and require the reprocessed archive to be open before the headline runs.

00:19:39.143 --> 00:19:48.591
<v Casey>Schweitzer's objection to the dinosaur paper fits here. Whole-bone chemistry and whole-archive reprocessing share a weakness: the average can hide the one thing you were looking for.

00:19:49.199 --> 00:19:58.015
<v Riley>Which is why the reprocessing should be checked by someone who didn't choose the settings. Someone with a different reason to get the answer right.

00:19:58.621 --> 00:20:04.869
<v Casey>Someone who loses nothing if the result is smaller than advertised. Those people are rarer than telescope time.

00:20:05.394 --> 00:20:17.922
<v Riley>I'll land where I started. FLIP and the Neptune work both look sound to me, and open archives plus planted-planet tests give them a route to discovery inside a few years. The Uranus probe can settle the rest.

00:20:18.570 --> 00:20:32.218
<v Casey>And I'll land on what's unproven. The hundreds of reruns, the second team, a probe that may not fly before the mid-2030s. Until one of those arrives, I'd call these leads, and I'd ask who's paying for each check before we call anything found.

00:20:32.742 --> 00:20:45.422
<v Riley>That's the question for anyone listening: when only one team can run the method, who should check it first? Tell us at feedback at cariboo signals dot c-a, especially if you've compared your own sky notes against a headline.

00:20:46.110 --> 00:20:51.830
<v Casey>With drizzle at Horsefly Lake today, your own check waits for a clear night. Enjoy the rest of your Sunday.

00:20:52.430 --> 00:21:10.222
